      "id": 1,
      "text": "Peggs was named for Thomas Pegg, who served as the acting principal chief of the Cherokee Nation during the Civil War.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peggs%2C_Oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The community of Peggs was officially established with the creation of its post office on December 6, 1899.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/peggs_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Peggs developed as a small rural settlement in the early twentieth century, serving local Cherokee Nation communities with stores and schools.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/peggs_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Many residents of Peggs are Native American, with most belonging to tribal groups like the Cherokee Nation and Muscogee Creek Nation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peggs%2C_Oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "New Deal projects from the WPA era helped stabilize the rural economy and modernize community buildings in Peggs.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
